  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Mechanistic path “CFTR → dysbiosis → inflammation”**\n\n**1. CFTR loss drives gut dysbiosis**  \n- **Animal (whole‑body CFTR‑KO)** – CFTR‑KO mice develop a distinct dysbiotic signature (↑Enterobacteriaceae, ↑Enterococcus) compared with WT controls【PMID:38554847】.  \n- **Animal (intestinal‑specific Cftr KO)** – iCftr‑KO mice show reproducible shifts toward Proteobacteria, Enterobacteriaceae and several Clostridia, together with reduced α‑diversity in one diet regimen【PMID:37546931】 (also confirmed in an independent cohort【PMID:38073491】).  \n- **Human (observational)** – Systematic review of 38 studies documents a consistent CF‑related dysbiosis (lower diversity, enrichment of pro‑inflammatory taxa) across pwCF【PMID:36600119】.  \n- **Human (intervention)** – Initiation of the CFTR potentiator ivacaftor increases the anti‑inflammatory genus *Akkermansia* and lowers Enterobacteriaceae, linking restored CFTR activity to microbiota normalization【PMID:30546102】.  \n\n*These data provide direct causal evidence (mouse KO) and strong association (human) that defective CFTR is sufficient to alter the gut microbial community.*\n\n**2. Dysbiosis contributes to intestinal (and systemic) inflammation**  \n- **Animal (CFTR‑KO with dysbiosis)** – The dysbiotic CFTR‑KO gut shows increased intestinal permeability and elevated mucosal inflammatory markers; non‑absorbable antibiotics that dampened dysbiosis also reduced permeability and liver inflammation【PMID:38554847】 (causal inference).  \n- **Animal (iCftr‑KO)** – Elevated fecal calprotectin (a neutrophil‑derived inflammation marker) co‑occurs with dysbiosis in both diet conditions, indicating that the microbial shift is coupled to intestinal inflammation【PMID:37546931】【PMID:38073491】.  \n- **Animal + in‑vitro (Bacteroides/propionate)** – Supplementing Bacteroides (propionate‑producing) in Cftr^F508del mice raises stool propionate and lowers systemic pro‑inflammatory cytokines; propionate directly"
  },
